<worksheet xml:id="ws-linear-differential-equations">
  <title>Linear Differential Equations</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Solving Linear DE using the integrating factor method.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <definition xml:id="LinearDE">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <alert>Linear Differential Equations</alert> takes the form
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt}+p(t)y=g(t)
        </md>
        where <m>p(t)</m> and <m>g(t)</m> are continuous functions.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    We are going to assume that there is some magical function somewhere out
    there in the world, <m>\mu(t)</m>, called an
    <alert>integrating factor</alert> (we will learn how to find it in a bit).
    We multiply our differential equation above by <m>\mu(t)</m> to get
    <md>
      \mu(t)\frac{dy}{dt}+\mu(t)\cdot p(t)y=\mu(t)\cdot g(t).
    </md>
    The magical thing about <m>\mu(t)</m> is that it will turn the left hand
    side into <m>\frac{d}{dt}\left[\mu(t)\cdot y\right]</m>, in other words
    <md>
      \frac{d}{dt}\left[\mu(t)\cdot y\right]=\mu(t)\frac{dy}{dt}+\mu(t)\cdot p(t)y.
    </md>
    If we think of the product rule this means that
    <md>
      \mu^{\prime}(t)=\mu(t)\cdot p(t).
    </md>
    We can solve this separable DE for <m>\mu(t)</m> to get
    <md>
      \mu(t)=e^{\int p(t)\,dt}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Now that we found this magical function <m>\mu(t)</m>, solving the
    differential equation
    <md>
      \frac{d}{dt}\left[\mu(t)\cdot y\right]=\mu(t)\cdot g(t)
    </md>
    involves an integration step (don't forget to add a <m>C</m> ), and then
    dividing by <m>mu(t)</m>.
  </p>

  <introduction>
    <title>Steps for solving a linear DE</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Put the differential equation in the correct initial form
            <md>
              \frac{dy}{dt}+p(t)y=g(t)
            </md>
            i.e the right hand side contains ONLY <m>t</m>, and the coefficient
            of <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m> is 1. So you can identify what <m>p(t)</m>
            and <m>g(t)</m> are.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Find the integrating factor <m>\mu(t)</m> using the formula
          </p>

          <p>
            <md>
              \mu(t)=e^{\int p(t)\,dt}
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Multiply everything in the differential equation by <m>\mu(t)</m>
            and verify that the left side becomes the product rule
            <m>\frac{d}{dt}\left[\mu(t)\cdot y\right]</m> and write it as such.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Integrate both sides, make sure you properly deal with the constant
            of integration.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Solve for the solution <m>y(t)</m> by dividing both sides by
            <m>\mu(t)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If you are given an initial condition, substitute it to find the the
            value of <m>C</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\displaystyle{\cos(t)y^\prime+\sin(t)y=2\cos^3(t)\sin(t)-1,\quad\quad\quad y\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=3\sqrt{2}.}</m>
    </p>
    <answer>
  <p>
    <md>\displaystyle{\cos(t)y^\prime+\sin(t)y=2\cos^3(t)\sin(t)-1,}</md>

    <md>\displaystyle{y^\prime+\tan(t)y=2\cos^2(t)\sin(t)-\sec(t),}</md>
    <md>
      <mrow>\mu(t) \amp= e^{\int \tan(t)\,dt}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp=e^{-\ln(\cos(t))}</mrow>
       <mrow>\amp=e^{\ln(\sec(t))}</mrow>
       <mrow>\amp=\sec(t)</mrow>
    </md>

    Now you multiply the equation by <m>\mu(t)</m> to get
    <md>\displaystyle{\sec(t)y^\prime+\sec(t)\cdot\tan(t)y=2\cos(t)\sin(t)-\sec^2(t)}</md>

    You can now verify that the left hand side can be written as 
    <md>\frac{d}{dt}( \sec(t) \cdot y)=2\cos(t)\sin(t)-\sec^2(t)</md>

      Ingrate both sides to get
      <md>\sec(t) \cdot y=\sin^2(t)-\tan(t)+C
      
      </md>
        Apply the initial condition to find C

        <md>\sec\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right) \cdot 3\sqrt{2}=\sin^2\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)-\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)+C
      
      </md>
      <md>3=\frac{1}{2}-1+C
      
      </md>
      <md>C=3.5</md>
      Hence our solution is given by
      <md>\sec\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right) \cdot 3\sqrt{2}=\sin^2\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)-\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{4}\right)+C
      
      </md>
  </p>
</answer>
  </example>

  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\displaystyle{ty^\prime-2y=t^5\sin(2t)-t^3+4t^4,\quad\quad\quad y\left(\pi\right)=\frac{3\pi^{4}}{2}.}</m>
    </p>
     <answer>
      <p>
        <md>\displaystyle{ty^\prime-2y=t^5\sin(2t)-t^3+4t^4}</md>

        <md>\displaystyle{y^\prime-\frac{2}{t}y=t^4\sin(2t)-t^2+4t^3}</md>

        <md>
      <mrow>\mu(t) \amp= e^{\int -\frac{2}{t}\,dt}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp=e^{-2\ln(t)}</mrow>
       <mrow>\amp=e^{\ln(t^{-2})}</mrow>
       <mrow>\amp=\frac{1}{t^2}</mrow>
    </md>

    <md>\frac{1}{t^2}y^\prime-\frac{2}{t^3}y=t^2\sin(2t)-1+4t.</md>

    You can now verify that the left hand side can be written as 
      <md>\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{1}{t^2}\cdot y\right)=t^2\sin(2t)-1+4t.</md>

      Ingrate both sides to get

       <md>\frac{1}{t^2}\cdot y=-t-\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\cos \left( 2\,t \right) +\frac{1}{4}\cos \left( 2\,t
 \right) +\frac{1}{2}t\sin \left( 2\,t \right) +2\,{t}^{2}
+C</md>

Apply the initial condition to find <m> C=\pi-{\pi}^{2}+\frac{1}{4}</m>
      </p>
      Hence our solution is given by
      <md> y=t^2\left(-t-\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\cos \left( 2\,t \right) +\frac{1}{4}\cos \left( 2\,t
 \right) +\frac{1}{2}t\sin \left( 2\,t \right) +2\,{t}^{2}
+\pi-{\pi}^{2}+\frac{1}{4}\right)</md>
    </answer>
  </example>


<exercise workspace="3.0in">
  <p>
  <m>\displaystyle{y^\prime-\frac{2}{t}y=2t^2,\quad\quad\quad y\left(-2\right)=4}</m>.
</p>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="3.0in">
  <p>
  <m>\displaystyle{y^\prime=-2ty+4e^{-t^2},\quad\quad\quad y\left(0\right)=3}</m>.
</p>
</exercise>
</worksheet>